<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian I, Prince of Anhalt-Bernburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1568">1568</a>–<a href="/years/1630">1630</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian I, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, received Bernburg in the 1603 division of Anhalt. A Calvinist statesman in the service of the Palatinate, he was a chief organiser of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> and the leading adviser of Frederick V in the Bohemian revolt. He commanded the army defeated at the White Mountain in 1620, and was later pardoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-i-prince-of-anhalt-bernburg.jpg" alt="Christian I, Prince of Anhalt-Bernburg" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Circle of Michiel van Miereveld · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christian_I._von_Anhalt-Bernburg,_1609.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christian was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> on 11 May 1568, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernest, Prince of Anhalt</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>, and his first wife Agnes, daughter of Wolfgang I, Count of Barby-Mühlingen. He was taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Italian and French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> by Caspar Gottschalk, and as a child took part in diplomatic missions, among them one to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In early 1586 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, where he spent several years as the closest friend of his namesake, Christian I, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, whose Calvinist sympathies he shared; his heavy drinking at the electoral court is recorded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A committed Calvinist, he became adviser to Frederick IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>. In 1591 he led the Palatine army in support of the French king Henry IV, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>' War over the bishopric of Strasbourg, which broke out in 1592, he backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. In 1595 Frederick IV made him Governor of the Upper Palatinate, and he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amberg">Amberg</a>. That year, at Lohrbach, he married Anna of Bentheim-Tecklenburg, with whom he had sixteen children; their son Christian II succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>.</p><p>By the division of 1603 he revived the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, extinct since 1468. As a diplomat he played an important part in founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> in 1608. He went on to serve Frederick V, and when the Bohemian nobles elected Frederick their king in 1619 he was given command of the Protestant forces defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> against Emperor Ferdinand II. The same year he was admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruitbearing-society">Fruitbearing Society</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat at White Mountain in 1620 he advised Frederick not to make a stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. In 1621 he was placed under the imperial ban, which made him an outlaw in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and stripped him of his lands. He fled first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and then to the court of King Christian IV of Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. In 1624 he appealed to Ferdinand for mercy and was allowed to return to his principality, where he died on 17 April 1630. His son Ernest, a cavalry colonel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> service, was fatally wounded at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> in 1632.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian I of Anhalt-Bernburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Principality of Anhalt was divided in 1603 among the heirs of Joachim Ernst, <b>Christian I</b> received Bernburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-bernburg">Principality of Anhalt-Bernburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.8,11.73&amp;year=1608" aria-label="Bernburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Christian I, Prince of Anhalt-Bernburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5616 5644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5616 5644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.8,11.73&amp;year=1608">What was here in 1608? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
